# Line endings, settled once -- same policy as baseball_predictor.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# Carried over from the sibling project, where 29 files showed as modified
# having had nothing done to them: 188 insertions and 188 deletions across
# four files, every line "changed", and `git diff --ignore-all-space`
# showing zero actual differences. The files were written to disk with CRLF
# while the repo held LF, and with core.autocrlf unset git treats that as
# real content.
#
# That is worse than cosmetic. A diff where every line changed hides the
# one line that actually did, and this project's whole debugging method is
# reading diffs carefully.
#
# Setting it here, on day one, means it never happens in this repo.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, stores it with LF in
# the repository, and checks it out with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception. setup.ps1 uses a here-string (@"..."@), and
# CRLF is the native convention for Windows-only scripts -- there is no
# Linux consumer of this file to keep byte-identical.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Explicitly binary, so git never tries to normalise or diff them as text.
*.parquet binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.joblib binary
*.pkl binary
